ck_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"After Nikki and Keith have had sex, they are lying together in bed. Nikki turns to Keith with her heart described as full and suddenly tells him she loves him; Keith pauses, smiles, and reciprocates the avowal.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal expression contains no identifiable undertaking by Nikki and therefore no undertaking requiring acceptance. Keith’s “I love you too” is reciprocal emotional uptake, not acceptance of specified commitment terms.\",\"recognised_work\":false,\"recognition_note\":\"I do not recognise the supplied passage as a known work.\",\"limitations\":\"The supplied evidence strongly supports the avowing and spontaneous-expressive forces at this moment, but it does not establish any precise relationship obligations created by the words themselves. It also cannot by itself settle the durability of either person’s feelings.\"}"
